<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yi Hwang</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1501">1501</a>–<a href="/years/1570">1570</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Toegye</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Yi Hwang, known as Toegye, was one of the two greatest Neo-Confucian philosophers of Joseon <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>. After a career as an official he retired to teach at his academy at Dosan, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andong">Andong</a>. His interpretation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhu-xi">Zhu Xi</a>, set out in works such as the Ten Diagrams on Sage Learning and in a famous debate on the Four-Seven question, influenced later Korean thought and Japanese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neo-confucianism">Neo-Confucianism</a>, and he appears on the South Korean 1,000-won note.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yi-hwang.jpg" alt="Yi Hwang" width="320" height="566" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Integral · CC BY-SA 2.0 kr · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Statue_of_Yi_Hwang.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Yi Hwang was born in 1501 in Ongye-ri, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andong">Andong</a>, in the north of Gyeongsang Province, the youngest of eight children of the Jinbo Yi clan. A child prodigy, he learned the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/analects">Analects of Confucius</a> from his uncle at twelve and, admiring the poetry of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tao-yuanming">Tao Yuanming</a>, began to write verse. His poem Yadang, written at eighteen, is counted among his major works. About the age of twenty he immersed himself in the I Ching and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neo-confucianism">Neo-Confucianism</a>. He came to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seoul">Seoul</a> at 23, entered the national academy Sungkyunkwan in 1523, and passed the civil service examinations with top honours in 1534.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Yi held office under four kings: Jungjong, Injong, Myeongjong and Seonjo. His integrity made him relentless in purges of corrupt officials, and on numerous occasions he was exiled from the capital for his commitment to principle. Disillusioned by the power struggles at court in the later years of Jungjong's reign, he left political office, but he was repeatedly drawn back. He served as governor of Danyang at 48 and then of Punggi, where he improved the private academy Baekundong Seowon.</p><p>He was named head instructor of Sungkyunkwan in 1552 but turned down other prominent offices. In 1560 he established the Dosan seodang, devoted to meditation, study and teaching. King Seonjo appointed him minister of rites, which he declined at first, but he resumed office at 68 and wrote advisory documents including the Ten Diagrams on Sage Learning. He retired at 70 and died in 1570, and was posthumously promoted to the highest ministerial rank. His disciples reorganised the Dosan seodang as Dosan Seowon in 1574.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Yi followed the dualistic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neo-confucianism">Neo-Confucianism</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhu-xi">Zhu Xi</a>, in which i and gi are the forces underlying the universe. He stressed i, the formative element, as the force that determines gi, in contrast to the school of his counterpart <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yi-i">Yi I</a>, which focused on gi. This emphasis became the core of the Yeongnam School, carried on by figures such as Yu Sŏngnyong and Kim Sŏngil.</p><p>The Ten Diagrams on Sage Learning, composed in 1568 for King Seonjo, presents the path to sagehood through ten chapters, each opening with a diagram and a text from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhu-xi">Zhu Xi</a> or another authority, and closing with a brief commentary. He also wrote sijo poetry. His thought influenced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan">Taiwan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>, and research institutes devoted to him exist in several countries.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Yi Hwang” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Neo-Confucian philosophy reached a high level in the debates of <b>Yi Hwang</b> (Toegye) and Yi I (Yulgok), whose ideas also influenced Japan, and later scholars of Practical Learning (silhak) such as Jeong Yak-yong turned to the reform of land, administration and technology.</blockquote><a href="/empire/joseon">Joseon · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>In Korea, scholars such as <b>Yi Hwang</b> and Yi I debated Neo-Confucian metaphysics in the 16th century.</blockquote><a href="/religion/confucianism">Confucianism · Texts &amp; Teachers</a></li><li><blockquote>A yangban was expected to study the Confucian classics rather than work with his hands, and many lived on country estates as teachers and local leaders, as <b>Yi Hwang</b> did at his academy near Andong.</blockquote><a href="/societies/joseon">Social Hierarchy of Joseon Korea</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andong">Andong</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/south-korea">South Korea</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andong">Andong</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/south-korea">South Korea</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=36.57,128.73&amp;year=1545" aria-label="Andong on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7785 845 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Yi Hwang"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8235 1115h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8235 1115h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=36.57,128.73&amp;year=1545">What was here in 1545? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
